<!-- ============================================================
     SonicWall SMA1000 SSRF + OS Command Injection Detection Rules
     CVE-2026-15409 (SSRF, CWE-918) + CVE-2026-15410 (Code Injection, CWE-77)
     Affected: SonicWall SMA1000 Secure Mobile Access appliances
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1059.004 - Unix Shell (TA0002 Execution)
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (TA0011 C2)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-14, Federal Deadline 2026-07-17
     Rule IDs: 103935-103944
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-14

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-15409 - SSRF (CVSS Critical):
       An unauthenticated remote attacker can craft HTTP requests to the
       SonicWall SMA1000 appliance that cause the appliance itself to
       initiate outbound HTTP connections to attacker-controlled destinations,
       including internal RFC1918 addresses and localhost. This allows the
       attacker to probe internal services, bypass network segmentation, and
       potentially reach internal APIs that are not exposed to the internet.
       Authentication is not required for exploitation.

     CVE-2026-15410 - Code Injection (CVSS High):
       An authenticated attacker with administrator credentials can inject
       arbitrary OS commands via specific parameters in the SMA1000 admin API.
       Under specific conditions, shell metacharacters embedded in admin API
       request parameters are passed unsanitized to an OS shell call, resulting
       in arbitrary command execution with the privileges of the SMA1000 web
       management daemon (typically root on the appliance).

     Detection Approach:

     CVE-2026-15409 SSRF (rules 103935-103940):
       Layer 1: HTTP/reverse-proxy logs (Traefik JSON format) for inbound
         requests to SMA1000 API paths containing RFC1918 addresses or SSRF
         protocol schemes in query parameters.
       Layer 2: SMA1000 native syslog forwarded to Wazuh - match SSRF payload
         patterns in management log messages.
       Layer 3: Firewall/netflow syslog - detect outbound connections FROM
         the SMA1000 appliance IP to internal RFC1918 destinations, which is
         the direct result of a successful SSRF exploit.
       Layer 4: Probe burst detection for automated exploit tool fingerprint.

     CVE-2026-15410 Code Injection (rules 103941-103944):
       Layer 1: HTTP/reverse-proxy logs for requests to SMA1000 admin API
         endpoints containing shell metacharacters or URL-encoded equivalents.
       Layer 2: Process execution logs on the SMA1000 (if Wazuh agent
         deployed) detecting shell processes spawned from the management daemon.

     Log Source Prerequisites:
       HTTP layer: Traefik access logs in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
         Field names: RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost,
         DownstreamStatus, ClientHost, rawRequest.
       Syslog layer: SMA1000 management logs forwarded via syslog UDP/TCP 514.
         Program name typically "smad", "webservd", or "sma1000d".
       Network/firewall layer: Perimeter firewall syslog showing source IP
         of SMA1000 appliance making outbound connections to internal hosts.
       Process layer (optional): Linux auditd or Wazuh FIM agent on SMA1000
         (if agent deployment is supported by the appliance OS version).

     Severity Tiers:
       CVE-2026-15409 SSRF:
         7  - Base anchor: request to SMA1000 API surface (correlation only)
        13  - RFC1918 or SSRF protocol in SMA1000 API request parameter
        14  - Localhost or cloud metadata service SSRF target
        12  - Syslog: SSRF payload in SMA1000 management log
        11  - Firewall: SMA1000 appliance IP initiating connection to RFC1918
         9  - Probe burst: rapid sequential requests to SMA1000 API
       CVE-2026-15410 Code Injection:
         7  - Base anchor: admin API endpoint request (correlation only)
        14  - Shell metacharacters in admin API request parameters
        15  - Shell process spawned from SMA1000 management daemon
        13  - URL-encoded command injection in admin API request

     False Positive Guidance:
       103935: SMA1000 management traffic from legitimate admin workstations;
         correlate with 103936-103937 for SSRF payload content.
       103936: RFC1918 addresses may legitimately appear in VPN routing API
         calls; review source IP and endpoint combination.
       103937: localhost/metadata SSRF has virtually no benign use in
         SMA1000 external API context; treat as high-confidence.
       103938: SMA1000 diagnostic tools may reference internal IPs in log
         output; correlate with inbound request timing.
       103939: SMA1000 may legitimately connect to internal identity providers
         (LDAP/RADIUS) or certificate servers; baseline known-good destinations
         and alert on deviations.
       103942: Admin API calls with URL-encoded characters are common; flag
         only when multiple shell metachar classes appear together or combined
         with unusual endpoint paths.
       103943: No benign explanation for a web management daemon spawning bash
         or sh directly; treat as confirmed code injection.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,sonicwall,sma1000,cisa_kev,cve_2026_15409,cve_2026_15410,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       CVE-2026-15409: SSRF Detection Layer 1 - HTTP/Traefik JSON
       Rule IDs: 103935-103940

       SMA1000 SSRF is exploited by sending HTTP requests to the
       appliance management or VPN API that include attacker-controlled
       URL components. The appliance then initiates outbound connections
       based on those parameters. Detection at the HTTP layer catches
       the exploit delivery before outbound SSRF connections fire.

       SMA1000 management API paths targeted in this rule set:
         /management/         - Primary management interface
         /api/v1/ /api/v2/    - REST API versions
         /vpn/                - VPN configuration endpoints
         /cgi-bin/            - Legacy CGI management scripts
         /webvpn/             - Web VPN access portal
         /cfg/                - Configuration API
         /diagnose/           - Diagnostic endpoints (SSRF-prone)
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: HTTP request to SMA1000 management or API path.
       Matches requests to the SMA1000 management surface in Traefik
       JSON access logs. This rule is level 7 and used as a correlation
       anchor; child rules 103936-103937 and 103944 chain from it via
       if_sid and raise severity when SSRF/injection payload is present. -->
  <rule id="103935" level="7">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/management/|/api/v[12]/|/vpn/|/cgi-bin/|/webvpn/|/cfg/|/diagnose/|/dana-admin/|/dana-na/auth/)</field>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000: HTTP request to management or API endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- CVE-2026-15409/15410 attack surface anchor (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_api_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: RFC1918 or SSRF protocol scheme in SMA1000 request.
       An attacker exploiting the SSRF vulnerability submits HTTP requests
       to SMA1000 API endpoints with internal IP addresses or SSRF-specific
       protocol schemes in URL parameters. The appliance then uses these
       values to initiate outbound connections to the specified targets.
       Matching RFC1918 ranges (10.x, 192.168.x, 172.16-31.x) or SSRF
       protocol schemes (gopher, dict, file, ftp) in the request path or
       query string is a high-confidence SSRF exploitation indicator. -->
  <rule id="103936" level="13">
    <if_sid>103935</if_sid>
    <field name="rawRequest" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|gopher://|dict://|file://|ftp://internal)</field>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: Request to $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) contains RFC1918 address or SSRF protocol scheme -- appliance will initiate outbound connection to internal target; active SSRF exploitation (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_ssrf,cve_2026_15409,internal_pivot,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: Localhost or cloud metadata service in SMA1000 request.
       Requests containing 127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost, or the cloud metadata
       endpoint 169.254.169.254 as SSRF targets represent the highest-confidence
       exploitation scenario. Localhost SSRF allows the attacker to reach SMA1000
       internal services (admin ports, health endpoints) not exposed externally.
       Metadata service SSRF (169.254.169.254) applies to cloud-hosted SMA1000
       deployments and enables credential theft from the instance metadata API. -->
  <rule id="103937" level="14">
    <if_sid>103935</if_sid>
    <field name="rawRequest" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:127\.0\.0\.1|localhost|::1|0\.0\.0\.0|169\.254\.169\.254|metadata\.internal|169\.254\.170\.2)</field>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15409 SSRF CRITICAL: Request to $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) targets localhost or cloud metadata service -- appliance SSRF to internal management interface or instance credential endpoint; immediate investigation required (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_ssrf,cve_2026_15409,localhost_ssrf,metadata_ssrf,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: SMA1000 syslog with SSRF payload indicators.
       For environments where SMA1000 management logs arrive via syslog
       rather than through a Traefik reverse proxy. Matches SMA1000 process
       names (smad, webservd, httpd) combined with internal address or SSRF
       protocol indicators in the log message. Covers cases where the
       appliance logs inbound request parameters before forwarding them. -->
  <rule id="103938" level="12">
    <program_name >(smad|webservd|sma1000d|httpd|ssl-vpn|sslvpnd)</program_name>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|127\.0\.0\.1|localhost|169\.254\.169\.254|gopher://|dict://|file://)</regex>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15409 SSRF syslog indicator on $(agent.name): Management daemon $(program_name) logged internal address or SSRF protocol scheme -- SMA1000 may be processing or forwarding attacker-controlled internal URL (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_ssrf,cve_2026_15409,syslog_variant,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: Outbound connection FROM SMA1000 IP to RFC1918 host.
       The definitive SSRF result signal is the SMA1000 appliance initiating
       TCP/UDP connections to internal RFC1918 addresses. This appears in
       perimeter firewall syslog as a connection where the source IP matches
       the SMA1000 appliance and the destination is an internal host. This
       rule matches common firewall syslog patterns (FortiGate, pfSense/OPNsense,
       iptables, and generic ALLOW log formats) where the SMA1000 is the source.
       Tune SMA1000_IP to the actual appliance management IP in your environment.
       Known-legitimate outbound connections (LDAP, RADIUS, NTP) should be
       baselined and suppressed via overrides after confirming SSRF is not active. -->
  <rule id="103939" level="11">
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:ALLOW|ACCEPT|permit|pass).*?(?:src|SRC|source|srcip)=(?:10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3})</regex>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:dst|DST|dest|dstip)=(?:10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3})</regex>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: Firewall log shows connection from internal source to internal RFC1918 destination on $(agent.name) -- if source IP is the SMA1000 appliance, this is the outbound SSRF connection; correlate source IP with SMA1000 appliance address (T1190/T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_ssrf,cve_2026_15409,ssrf_outbound,firewall_log,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15409 SSRF: Rapid probe burst against SMA1000 API endpoints.
       Automated exploit tools and scanners issue high-frequency requests to
       enumerate vulnerable SMA1000 API paths before SSRF payload delivery.
       Matching 10 or more requests from the same source within a short window
       to the SMA1000 API surface is consistent with scanner or exploit
       framework reconnaissance activity. -->
  <rule id="103940" level="9" frequency="10" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103935</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000: Rapid probe burst from $(ClientHost) to management/API endpoints on $(RequestHost) -- 10+ requests matched CVE-2026-15409 attack surface; exploit scanner or automated exploitation tool fingerprint (T1190)</description>
    <options>alert_by_email</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_ssrf,cve_2026_15409,probe_burst,scanner,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       CVE-2026-15410: Code Injection Detection Layer - Admin API
       Rule IDs: 103941-103944

       CVE-2026-15410 requires admin authentication. An authenticated
       admin submits API requests to SMA1000 configuration or diagnostic
       endpoints with shell metacharacters embedded in parameter values.
       Under specific conditions the appliance passes these parameters
       to an OS shell call without sanitization, resulting in arbitrary
       command execution as the web daemon user (root).

       Key detection signals:
         1. Admin API requests with unencoded shell metacharacters
         2. Admin API requests with URL-encoded shell metacharacter sequences
         3. Shell/interpreter process spawned from SMA1000 management daemon
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: HTTP request to SMA1000 admin configuration or diagnostic API.
       Matches requests to endpoints known to be injectable in CVE-2026-15410.
       SMA1000 admin endpoints include configure paths, firmware/upgrade paths,
       diagnostic command execution endpoints, and certificate management.
       Level 7 anchor used for correlation; child rules raise severity. -->
  <rule id="103941" level="7">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/management/configure/|/api/v[12]/admin/|/api/v[12]/system/|/cfg/|/diagnose/|/management/upgrade/|/management/certificate/|/management/diagnostic/|/api/v[12]/network/)</field>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000: HTTP request to admin configuration API $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) -- CVE-2026-15410 code injection attack surface anchor; monitor for shell metacharacters in parameters (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_admin_api,cve_2026_15410,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15410: Shell metacharacters in SMA1000 admin API request.
       Shell metacharacters embedded in admin API parameter values indicate
       an attempt to inject OS commands via CVE-2026-15410. The vulnerable
       parameter handling passes values unsanitized to a shell call.
       Pattern matches: semicolon (;), pipe (|), backtick (`), dollar-paren
       command substitution ($(), newline injection (%0a/%0d), and the
       double-ampersand URL-encoded form (%26%26). These characters have no
       legitimate use in SMA1000 admin API parameter values and their presence
       is a strong indicator of command injection attempt. -->
  <rule id="103942" level="14">
    <if_sid>103941</if_sid>
    <field name="rawRequest" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:[;|`]|\$\(|\$\{|%3[Bb]|%7[Cc]|%60|%24%28|%24%7[Bb]|%0[Aa]|%0[Dd]|%26%26|%7[Cc]%7[Cc])</field>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15410 Code Injection: Admin API request to $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) contains shell metacharacters or URL-encoded command injection sequences -- active OS command injection attempt via admin API; immediate investigation required (T1059.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_code_injection,cve_2026_15410,shell_metachar,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15410: Shell/interpreter process spawned from SMA1000 web daemon.
       The definitive post-exploitation signal for CVE-2026-15410 is an OS shell
       or interpreter process spawned with the SMA1000 web management daemon as
       parent. SMA1000 management daemons (smad, webservd, httpd, sslvpnd) have
       no legitimate reason to exec a shell directly. A bash/sh/python/perl child
       of any of these processes confirms successful OS command injection.
       This rule matches syslog or auditd events where the parent process is a
       known SMA1000 management daemon and the child is an interactive shell or
       general-purpose interpreter. -->
  <rule id="103943" level="15">
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:smad|webservd|sma1000d|sslvpnd|vpnd|httpd).*?(?:execve|exec|spawn|child).*?(?:/bin/bash|/bin/sh|/bin/dash|/usr/bin/python|/usr/bin/perl|/usr/bin/ruby|bash|sh\s|dash)</regex>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15410 Code Injection CONFIRMED: Shell or interpreter process spawned from SMA1000 management daemon on $(agent.name) -- successful OS command injection via CVE-2026-15410; immediate incident response required (T1059.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_code_injection,cve_2026_15410,shell_spawn,critical,high_confidence,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- CVE-2026-15410: URL-encoded command injection in SMA1000 admin API.
       Attackers may encode shell metacharacters to bypass WAF or basic input
       filters while still delivering a command injection payload. This rule
       targets the SMA1000 syslog variant where admin API requests with
       encoded injection sequences appear in management log output rather than
       in Traefik JSON access logs. Matches program names of SMA1000 daemons
       combined with URL-encoded or hex-encoded shell injection patterns. -->
  <rule id="103944" level="13">
    <program_name >(smad|webservd|sma1000d|sslvpnd|httpd)</program_name>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:configure|diagnostic|upgrade|certificate|network).*?(?:%3[Bb]|%7[Cc]|%60|%24%28|%0[Aa]|%26%26|\\x3b|\\x7c|\\x60)</regex>
    <description>SonicWall SMA1000 CVE-2026-15410 Code Injection syslog: Management daemon $(program_name) on $(agent.name) logged admin API request to configuration endpoint with URL-encoded shell metacharacters -- command injection via CVE-2026-15410; investigate for shell execution (T1059.004/T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sma1000_code_injection,cve_2026_15410,encoded_injection,syslog_variant,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
